To ensure that your `NavigationDrawer` stays consistent while switching between fragments in your existing `MainActivity`, you will need to implement fragment transactions within the `onNavigationItemSelected` method. Below are steps to guide you on how to achieve this:

1. **Replace the Fragment on Navigation Item Click**: In your `onNavigationItemSelected` method, use `FragmentManager` to replace the current fragment with the selected fragment. For instance:

    ```java
    @Override
    public boolean onNavigationItemSelected(MenuItem item) {
        int id = item.getItemId();
        Fragment fragment = null;

        if (id == R.id.gps_overview) {
            fragment = new GPSOverviewFragment(); // Replace with your fragment class
        } else if (id == R.id.tachoscheibe) {
            fragment = new TachometerFragment(); // Replace with your fragment class
        } else if (id == R.id.settings) {
            fragment = new SettingsFragment(); // Replace with your fragment class
        }

        if (fragment != null) {
            FragmentManager fragmentManager = getSupportFragmentManager();
            fragmentManager.beginTransaction().replace(R.id.container, fragment).commit();
        }

        DrawerLayout drawer = (DrawerLayout) findViewById(R.id.drawer_layout);
        drawer.closeDrawer(GravityCompat.START);
        return true;
    }
    ```

   By using this code, when an item in the `NavigationDrawer` is clicked, the corresponding fragment is loaded into the specified container (`R.id.container`). This keeps the NavigationDrawer visible.

2. **Initial Fragment Load**: If you want to display a default fragment when the activity starts, you can add this code in your `onCreate` method:

    ```java
    if (savedInstanceState == null) {
        FragmentManager fragmentManager = getSupportFragmentManager();
        fragmentManager.beginTransaction().replace(R.id.container, new GPSOverviewFragment()).commit(); // Default fragment
    }
    ```

3. **Update ActionBar Title**: You may also want to update the ActionBar title when different fragments are selected:

    ```java
    getSupportActionBar().setTitle("New Title"); // Set to corresponding fragment title
    ```

   This is similar to updating the ActionBar title with the fragment name, as noted in [SO-44728425].

4. **Drawer State Management**: To manage the drawer state (for example, closing it when a navigation item is selected), make sure to invoke `drawer.closeDrawer(GravityCompat.START);` after performing fragment transactions, as shown above.

These steps ensure that your navigation drawer remains intact across different fragments, providing a seamless user experience while navigating your application. The sources detailed in this approach draw from best practices found in the provided context.

