Q: Performance impact of changing to generic interfaces
A: <p>In general, I'd say if you are replace the equivalent non-generic interface by the generic flavour (say <code>IList&lt;&gt;</code> --> <code>IList&lt;T&gt;</code>) you are bound to get <em>better</em> or equivalent performance. </p>

<p>One unique selling point is that because, unlike java, .NET does not use type erasure <em>and</em> supports true value types (<code>struct</code>), one of the main differences would be in how it stores e.g. a <code>List&lt;int&gt;</code> internally. This could quite quickly become a big difference depending on how intensively the List is being used.</p>

<hr>

<p>A braindead synthetic benchmark showed:</p>

<pre><code>    for (int j=0; j&lt;1000; j++)
    {
        List&lt;int&gt; list = new List&lt;int&gt;();
        for (int i = 1&lt;&lt;12; i&gt;0; i--)
            list.Add(i);

        list.Sort();
    }
</code></pre>

<p>to be faster by a factor of <strong>3.2x</strong> than the semi-equivalent non-generic:</p>

<pre><code>    for (int j=0; j&lt;1000; j++)
    {
        ArrayList list = new ArrayList();
        for (int i = 1&lt;&lt;12; i&gt;0; i--)
            list.Add(i);

        list.Sort();
    }
</code></pre>

