[SO-10963898] Q: Should I implement non-generic GetHashCode and Equals if my class implements IEqualityComparer<T>?
A: <p>It depends.  <code>IEqualityComparer&lt;T&gt;</code> is used to compare two instances of a type <code>T</code> - and typically implemented by a separate comparer class.  Normally you wouldn't not implement this in the class type <code>T</code>.  It's meant to provide an <em>alternative</em> comparison for use with types that support his.</p>

<p>If you're implementing this within the class itself, you'd typically implement <a href="http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/ms131187.aspx" rel="nofollow"><code>IEquatable&lt;T&gt;</code></a>.</p>

<p>That being said, it's often useful to override <code>Object.Equals</code>, which is typically very simple as you can use the <code>IEquatable&lt;T&gt;.Equals</code> method to implement <code>Object.Equals</code>.  This makes it "inexpensive" to implement.  As <code>Object.Equals</code> may be used, it will provide a consistent meaning to equality, so it's generally a good idea to implement.</p>

<p>If your object is going to be used as a key in a hash, such as a <code>Dictionary&lt;T,U&gt;</code> or a <code>HashSet&lt;T&gt;</code>, then you should override <code>GetHashCode</code>.  If there's even a chance that it may be used in this manner, overriding this method is beneficial.  Typically, I find that it's useful to override <code>GetHashCode</code> any time I implement equality, just in case I will use the type as a key later.</p>


