The retrieved sources do not cover how to specifically get a unique selector in jQuery when there are multiple elements with the same selector. However, to select the second instance of an element, you can use the `eq()` method or the `:nth-of-type()` selector in jQuery. 

For example, if you want to select the second `<td>` within a specific `<tr>`, you could modify your code to look like this:

```javascript
const data = $('.row table tr').map(function() {
  const a = $(this).find('td:first-child a');
  const td = $(this).find('td:last-child').eq(1); // Selecting the second <td>

  return {
    version: a.text(),
    href: a.attr('href'),
    date: td.text()
  }
}).get();
console.log(data);
```

Alternatively, you could use:

```javascript
const data = $('.row table tr').map(function() {
  const a = $(this).find('td:first-child a');
  const td = $(this).find('td:last-child:nth-of-type(2)'); // Using nth-of-type selector

  return {
    version: a.text(),
    href: a.attr('href'),
    date: td.text()
  }
}).get();
console.log(data);
```

This will target the specific instance you need.

