To display the `line_items` from the provided JSON response in a `RecyclerView` using a `CardView` and the Volley library, follow these steps:

1. **Set up the RecyclerView**: 
   Ensure that you have a `RecyclerView` in your layout XML where you want to display the items. Also, create a layout for each item in your RecyclerView, typically using a `CardView` to mimic the card layout.

2. **Create the Data Model**: 
   Define a data model class that matches the structure of the items you will be displaying. For your `line_items`, you could create a class like this:

   ```java
   public class LineItem {
       private int id;
       private String name;
       private double price;

       public LineItem(int id, String name, double price) {
           this.id = id;
           this.name = name;
           this.price = price;
       }

       // Getters for the properties
       public int getId() { return id; }
       public String getName() { return name; }
       public double getPrice() { return price; }
   }
   ```

3. **Set up the Adapter**: 
   Create an adapter that extends `RecyclerView.Adapter` to bind your data model to the RecyclerView. You will need to override `onCreateViewHolder`, `onBindViewHolder`, and `getItemCount` methods. Here is an example:

   ```java
   public class LineItemAdapter extends RecyclerView.Adapter<LineItemAdapter.ViewHolder> {
       private List<LineItem> lineItems;

       public LineItemAdapter(List<LineItem> lineItems) {
           this.lineItems = lineItems;
       }

       @Override
       public ViewHolder onCreateViewHolder(ViewGroup parent, int viewType) {
           View view = LayoutInflater.from(parent.getContext()).inflate(R.layout.item_line, parent, false);
           return new ViewHolder(view);
       }

       @Override
       public void onBindViewHolder(ViewHolder holder, int position) {
           LineItem item = lineItems.get(position);
           holder.nameTextView.setText(item.getName());
           holder.priceTextView.setText(String.valueOf(item.getPrice()));
       }

       @Override
       public int getItemCount() {
           return lineItems.size();
       }

       public static class ViewHolder extends RecyclerView.ViewHolder {
           TextView nameTextView;
           TextView priceTextView;

           public ViewHolder(View itemView) {
               super(itemView);
               nameTextView = itemView.findViewById(R.id.textView_name);
               priceTextView = itemView.findViewById(R.id.textView_price);
           }
       }
   }
   ```

4. **Fetch Data using Volley**:
   After configuring the RecyclerView and the adapter, use Volley to fetch your JSON data. You need to parse the JSON response to extract the `line_items` and create a list of `LineItem` objects.

   ```java
   RequestQueue requestQueue = Volley.newRequestQueue(this);
   String url = "your_api_endpoint_here"; // URL to your JSON response

   JsonObjectRequest jsonObjectRequest = new JsonObjectRequest(Request.Method.GET, url, null,
       new Response.Listener<JSONObject>() {
           @Override
           public void onResponse(JSONObject response) {
               try {
                   JSONArray lineItemsArray = response.getJSONArray("line_items");
                   List<LineItem> lineItems = new ArrayList<>();

                   for (int i = 0; i < lineItemsArray.length(); i++) {
                       JSONObject itemObject = lineItemsArray.getJSONObject(i);
                       int id = itemObject.getInt("id");
                       String name = itemObject.getString("name");
                       double price = itemObject.getDouble("price");
                       lineItems.add(new LineItem(id, name, price));
                   }

                   LineItemAdapter adapter = new LineItemAdapter(lineItems);
                   recyclerView.setAdapter(adapter);

               } catch (JSONException e) {
                   e.printStackTrace();
               }
           }
       },
       new Response.ErrorListener() {
           @Override
           public void onErrorResponse(VolleyError error) {
               // Handle the error
           }
       }
   );

   requestQueue.add(jsonObjectRequest);
   ```

5. **Other Considerations**:
   Make sure you have added the required permissions for internet access in your `AndroidManifest.xml`, and included the necessary dependencies for Volley in your `build.gradle` file.

