To create a NumericTextBox in .NET that ensures only valid numeric input, you could either extend the existing TextBox control or implement a validation rule as part of your WPF application.

1. **Extending the TextBox Control**: You can create a custom control by extending the `TextBox`. In doing so, you can override the `OnTextChanged` event and implement your validation logic. Here's a basic example of this approach:

   ```csharp
   public class NumericTextBox : TextBox
   {
       public NumericTextBox() : base()
       {
           TextChanged += OnTextChanged;
       }

       public void OnTextChanged(object sender, TextChangedEventArgs changed)
       {
           if (!String.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Text))
           {
               try
               {
                   int value = Convert.ToInt32(Text); // Adjust as necessary for different numeric types.
               }
               catch (Exception e)
               {
                   MessageBox.Show(String.Format("{0} only accepts numeric input.", Name));
                   Text = ""; // Clear non-numeric text
               }
           }
       }
   }
   ```

   This implementation allows the control to show a message if the input is invalid and clears the `Text` property if an exception occurs due to invalid input ([SO-1268552]).

2. **Using Validation Rules**: Alternatively, you can use validation rules with data binding. You would define a validation rule to check that only numeric values are allowed. Here's a simple example:

   ```xml
   <TextBox>
       <TextBox.Text>
           <Binding Path="OnyDigitInput" Mode="TwoWay" UpdateSourceTrigger="PropertyChanged">
               <Binding.ValidationRules>
                   <conv:OnlyDigitsValidationRule />
               </Binding.ValidationRules>
           </Binding>
       </TextBox.Text>
   </TextBox>
   ```

   The `OnlyDigitsValidationRule` could use regular expressions to validate its input:

   ```csharp
   public class OnlyDigitsValidationRule : ValidationRule
   {
       public override ValidationResult Validate(object value, CultureInfo cultureInfo)
       {
           var validationResult = new ValidationResult(true, null);
           if (value != null)
           {
               if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(value.ToString()))
               {
                   var regex = new Regex("[^0-9.-]+");
                   var parsingOk = !regex.IsMatch(value.ToString());
                   if (!parsingOk)
                   {
                       validationResult = new ValidationResult(false, "Illegal Characters, Please Enter Numeric Value");
                   }
               }
           }
           return validationResult;
       }
   }
   ```

   This approach allows for more flexibility and leverages WPF's binding capabilities ([SO-1268552]).

3. **PreviewTextInput Event**: Another technique is to handle the PreviewTextInput event, which allows you to control input before it is added to the TextBox:

   ```csharp
   private void TextBox_OnPreviewTextInput(object sender, TextCompositionEventArgs e)
   {
       var textBox = sender as TextBox;
       var fullText = textBox.Text.Insert(textBox.SelectionStart, e.Text);
       double val;
       e.Handled = !double.TryParse(fullText, out val);
   }
   ```

   This method can validate input as users type, effectively preventing the entry of non-numeric characters ([SO-1268552]).

