The issue you're encountering with your custom Adapter, where some items incorrectly show the favorite icon, is likely related to view recycling in Android's ListView. The recycled views can hold onto the visibility state set for one item when they are used for another item.

To fix this issue, you need to make sure that all states of your views are properly reset in the `GetView` method. Specifically, you should ensure that the visibility of the favorite icon is explicitly set for every item, not just when it's favored. Here’s a modification to your existing `GetView` method:

```csharp
public override View GetView(int position, View convertView, ViewGroup parent)
{
    View row = convertView;
    if (row == null)
        row = LayoutInflater.From(context).Inflate(Resource.Layout.modRow, null, false);

    // Set the text for the mod item
    row.FindViewById<TextView>(Resource.Id.textView1).Text = items[position].name;
    row.FindViewById<TextView>(Resource.Id.textView2).Text = items[position].game;

    // Reset the visibility of the favorite icon for the current item
    var favIcon = row.FindViewById<ImageView>(Resource.Id.favIcon);
    favIcon.Visibility = items[position].favorited ? ViewStates.Visible : ViewStates.Gone;

    return row;
}
```

In the code above, the visibility of the favorite icon is explicitly set to `ViewStates.Gone` if the item is not favored. This ensures that when a view is recycled, it does not mistakenly keep the visibility state of a previous item, which can lead to the issue you're describing. This approach is similar to suggestions found in the context regarding how to handle the ViewHolder pattern and proper tagging of views to manage their state [SO-35123685].

Remember to check your data size with `getCount()` for good practice, as this helps ensure that your adapter behaves correctly and only processes as many items as it should [SO-35123685]. 

