Q: How to Pass (void**) to Function and Efficiently Dereference/Use for Any type?
A: <p>This smells a bit like an XY problem and the code duplication isn't too fresh either. So I recommend using a different strategy altogether. Use callbacks instead.</p>

<p>The <code>mtrx_prnv</code> function iterates over all indices, but uses a provided callback to print the actual values. The callback receives the indices and the matrix as a <code>void *</code> (just like <code>mtrx_prnv</code> itself), but converts it to the appropriate type and prints the value.</p>

<pre><code>void mtrx_prnv(size_t n, size_t m, void * mtrx, void (*prncb)(size_t i, size_t j, void * mtrx)){
    size_t i, j;
    for (i = 0; i &lt; m; i++) {
        printf(" [ ");
        for (j = 0; j &lt; n; j++) {
            prncb(i, j, mtrx); /* Use callback to print value */
            printf(", "); /* fix the trailing comma yourself ;) */
        }
        printf (" ]\n");
    }
}
</code></pre>

