To show an error message when a user enters the wrong password in your Django view, you can modify your `login` function to handle unsuccessful authentication. Instead of redirecting the user back to the login page after a failed login attempt, you can render the login page again with an error message.

Here's how you can adjust your `login` view function:

```python
def login(request):
    if request.method == "GET":
        return render(request, 'users/login.html')
    else:
        Email = request.POST.get('Email')
        Senha = request.POST.get('Senha')
        user = authenticate(username=Email, password=Senha)

        if user:
            loginDjango(request, user)
            return render(request, 'convite/cadastro_convite.html')
        else:
            # Prepare an error message when login fails
            error_message = 'Invalid username or password.'
            return render(request, 'users/login.html', {'error': error_message})
```

In your `login.html` template, you can then display this error message if it exists:

```html
{% if error %}
    <div class="alert alert-danger">
        {{ error }}
    </div>
{% endif %}
```

The above modifications will show an error message for invalid login attempts without having to use a Django form. This approach is consistent with handling login failures directly in function-based views, as indicated by discussions around regular error handling. 

