To fix the issue where the Up Navigation (Action Bar's back arrow) is not functioning correctly for fragments in your application, you can follow these steps based on the provided context:

1. **Set Navigation Click Listener**: You need to ensure that you are setting a navigation click listener for your toolbar after you initialize it in your activity. This listener should handle the action of going back when the up button is pressed. Here's how you can do it:

   ```java
   toolbar.setNavigationOnClickListener(new View.OnClickListener() {
       @Override
       public void onClick(View v) {
           if (mFragmentManager.getBackStackEntryCount() > 0) {
               mFragmentManager.popBackStack();
           } else {
               onBackPressed(); // This will call the default back action
           }
       }
   });
   ```

   This pattern ensures that if there are fragments in the back stack, they are popped. Otherwise, the default back action is executed. This is supported by [SO-47068168].

2. **Enable Back Navigation Only When Needed**: Inside your fragments, you need to enable the Up Navigation when the fragment is pushed to the back stack. When you enter the second fragment, you can set the `setDisplayHomeAsUpEnabled(true)` method to enable the back button:

   In your `TripListFragment`, you can ensure it looks like this:

   ```java
   @Override
   public void onResume() {
       super.onResume();
       ActionBar actionBar = mActivity.getSupportActionBar();
       if (actionBar != null) {
           actionBar.setDisplayHomeAsUpEnabled(true);
       }
   }
   ```

   And don't forget to reset it in `CategoryFragment`:

   ```java
   @Override
   public void onResume() {
       super.onResume();
       ActionBar actionBar = mActivity.getSupportActionBar();
       if (actionBar != null) {
           actionBar.setDisplayHomeAsUpEnabled(false);
       }
   }
   ```

   This logic helps ensure that the up button works correctly depending on which fragment is active. This concept is highlighted in [SO-48185871].

3. **Fragment Back Stack Listener**: It may also be beneficial to listen for changes to the back stack. You can achieve this by adding a back stack changed listener in your `onCreate` method:

   ```java
   getSupportFragmentManager().addOnBackStackChangedListener(new FragmentManager.OnBackStackChangedListener() {
       @Override
       public void onBackStackChanged() {
           if (getSupportFragmentManager().getBackStackEntryCount() > 0) {
               toolbar.setNavigationOnClickListener(new View.OnClickListener() {
                   @Override
                   public void onClick(View view) {
                       mFragmentManager.popBackStack();
                   }
               });
           } else {
               toolbar.setNavigationOnClickListener(null); // Disable back action if no fragments in stack
           }
       }
   });
   ```

   This approach is also mentioned in the context, as it helps to manage the state of the toolbar's navigation based on the back stack content [SO-48185871].

4. **Handle Drawer Layout**: To prevent the drawer from being swiped open in the second fragment, you can lock the drawer from being opened depending on the active fragment.  You'll want to update the drawer's lock mode when switching between fragments, ensuring that it is only enabled for the first fragment.

Following these steps should help you resolve the issue of the Up Navigation not working properly in your application. If there are any other specific issues that do not fit this solution, the retrieved sources do not cover that part.

