Q: update progress indicator within javascript function
A: <p>How about this?</p>

<pre><code>var height = 300;
var width = 1000;
var totalpixels = height * width; //used for calculating loading percentages;
var currentpixel = 0; //used for calculating loading percentages;

incrementAndUpdate(currentpixel, totalpixels);

function incrementAndUpdate(currentValue, totalPixels) {
    $("#log").html((currentValue / totalPixels) * 100 + "%");
    if (currentValue &lt; totalPixels) {
        setTimeout(function () { incrementAndUpdate(currentValue + 1, totalPixels); }, 1);
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>​</p>

<p>Given the slow speed of this, I suggest that you maybe call this with less frequency and do more work units with each attempt at a setTimeout/html update call.  Maybe create a queue of work to do, and in your <code>incrementAndUpdate</code> function, choose to either call the function directly, or via setTimeout.</p>

<p>Something like so...</p>

<pre><code>function incrementAndUpdate(currentValue, totalPixels) {
    $("#log").html((currentValue / totalPixels) * 100 + "%");

    // do a bit of work.

    if (currentValue &lt; totalPixels) {
        var workFunction = function () { incrementAndUpdate(currentValue + 1, totalPixels); }
        if (currentValue % 100 == 0)
            setTimeout(workFunction, 1);
        else
            workFunction();
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>This does the same amount of work, but it does it faster with fewer <code>setTimeout</code> calls.  You could tweak the frequency with which the setTimeout is called in order to optimize the user experience as much as possible.</p>

