Q: How do I force an html5 embedded video to finish playing?
A: <blockquote>
  <p>the goal there is to force the video to finish before the learner can advance to the next slide.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Not using JavaScript in a case like this is like expecting a pizza delivered without ordering one (and JavaScript is really not a cross-platform issue).</p>

<p>The only way the video element can tell the browser environment that it has finished (excluding the visuals/UI) is to broadcast the event for <code>ended</code>. To pick up this event you need JavaScript.</p>

<p>On an ended event you can enable a next button or something of that sort:</p>

<h1>Example</h1>

<p>The video below will skip to 8:04 minutes (in browsers that support media fragments - so you don't need to skip to the end, not a requirement) and when the video ends the button above it named NEXT will be enabled -</p>

<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>document.querySelector("video").addEventListener("ended", function() {
  // enable NEXT button when video finished:
  document.querySelector("button").disabled = false;
})</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>body {background:#999}
button:disabled {color:#ccc}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;button disabled=true&gt;NEXT&lt;/button&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;video autoplay width="720" height="406" controls src="http://download.atsmedia.com/LMS/PGE/COUB-Sale.mp4#t=484" type="video/mp4"&gt;
&lt;/video&gt;</code

Q: HTML5 Video run when completely visible
A: <p>You can add an event listener to the window "scroll" event. There you can check whether the _video element is "in view" by  passing it to the isScrolledIntoView function. If it is, play() the _video, else pause(). <a href="http://jsbin.com/tizuve/edit?js,output" rel="nofollow">Example here.</a></p>

<p><strong>JS</strong></p>

<pre><code>var _video = document.querySelector('video');

function isScrolledIntoView( element ) {
    var elementTop    = element.getBoundingClientRect().top,
        elementBottom = element.getBoundingClientRect().bottom;

    return elementTop &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; elementBottom &lt;= window.innerHeight;
}

window.addEventListener("scroll", function(){
  if (isScrolledIntoView(_video)) {
    _video.play();
  }
  else {
    _video.pause()
  }
})
</code></pre>

<p><strong>HTML</strong></p>

<pre><code>&lt;div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;video&gt;
  &lt;source src="http://www.w3schools.com/html/mov_bbb.mp4" type="video/mp4"&gt;
  Your browser does not support HTML5 video.
&lt;/video&gt;
&lt;div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Html5 Video Not Working Chrome
A: <p>If your <code>img</code> folder is inside the same folder where your HTML is. You should remove the first slash. Try this out: </p>

<pre><code>&lt;video width="1920" height="1080" controls autoplay id="video-background"&gt; 
    &lt;source src="img/reel.mp4" type="video/mp4"&gt;
    &lt;source src="img/reel.webm" type="video/webm"&gt;
    Sorry, your browser doesn't support HTML5 video.
&lt;/video&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Hope it helps!</p>

