To enable horizontal drag detection on mobile devices, you will need to use touch events, as pointer events may not work the same way across different devices. You can modify your existing code to handle both pointer and touch events to ensure compatibility on mobile devices.

Here's an approach to refactor your code:

1. Handle both touch and pointer events to detect movements on mobile devices seamlessly.
2. Use touch events (`touchstart`, `touchmove`, and `touchend`) alongside pointer events.

Here’s the modified code:

```javascript
const myObject = document.getElementById("scroll-window");
play3D();
var a = 0;
var b = 0;

function onStart(e) {
    // For touch events, get the first touch point
    if (e.touches) {
        a = e.touches[0].clientX;
    } else {
        a = e.clientX;
    }
}

function onMove(e) {
    e.preventDefault(); // Prevent scrolling while dragging
    if (e.touches) {
        b = e.touches[0].clientX;
    } else {
        b = e.clientX;
    }
    var motionx = e.movementX;
    if (motionx >= 10){
        motionx = 10
    }
    var moveX = b - a;
    if (moveX > 5) {
        console.log(moveX);
        setImage(+1);
        a = b;
    } else if (moveX < -5) {
        setImage(-1);
    }
    a = b;
}

function onEnd() {
    moveX = 0;
    myObject.onpointermove = null;
    myObject.ontouchmove = null; // Clear touch move event
}

// Use both pointer events and touch events
myObject.onpointerdown = onStart;
myObject.onpointermove = onMove;
myObject.onpointerup = onEnd;

myObject.ontouchstart = onStart;
myObject.ontouchmove = onMove;
myObject.ontouchend = onEnd;
```

Changes made:

- Added `ontouchstart`, `ontouchmove`, and `ontouchend` to handle touch interactions.
- Used `e.touches` to get the position of the touch for identifying the initial and current touch positions.
- Prevented default scrolling behavior during the drag using `e.preventDefault()`.

This approach should make your drag detection work on both mobile devices and desktop environments. The sources provided do not explicitly address the incorporation of touch events but emphasize the need for detection of various user inputs and events, which you can apply here.

