Q: How best to signal dequeue caller that call is now bridged with Twilio?
A: <p>Twilio Developer Evangelist here,</p>

<p>It looks like the problem you're having could be solved by adding in some more <a href="https://www.twilio.com/docs/api/twiml?utm_source=stackoverflow&amp;utm_medium=june2015&amp;utm_campaign=stackathon" rel="nofollow">Twiml verbs</a> to alert the caller. You can do this with the <a href="https://www.twilio.com/docs/api/twiml/say&amp;utm_medium=june2015&amp;utm_campaign=stackathon" rel="nofollow">Say</a> command by having a robot voice read text over the phone line, or by using the <a href="https://www.twilio.com/docs/api/twiml/play&amp;utm_medium=june2015&amp;utm_campaign=stackathon" rel="nofollow">Play</a> command to play a prerecorded sound. </p>

<p>An example of this using some raw Twiml could look like:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;Response&gt;
    &lt;Say&gt;You are now being bridged to a caller&lt;/Say&gt;
    &lt;Dial&gt;
        &lt;Queue url="about_to_connect.xml"&gt;support&lt;/Queue&gt;
    &lt;/Dial&gt;
&lt;/Response&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>If you need any more help with the Queue verb, you can check out our <a href="https://www.twilio.com/docs/howto/callqueue&amp;utm_medium=june2015&amp;utm_campaign=stackathon" rel="nofollow">Call Queue How-To</a>, which includes example code and Twiml.</p>

Q: Twilio connecting an agent to a call in a queue
A: <p>So, after some more research, I have this working now. Since I already started a bounty, I'll update with the answer that worked for me...</p>

<p>My <code>javascript</code> "Connect Agent to Call" button should NOT have been calling my own endpoint to connect. Here's what it changed to:</p>

<pre><code>Twilio.Device.connect({ To: 'Support', From: 'Agent' });
</code></pre>

<p>Then, that routes the call back through my "Call" controller, where I had to add this logic:</p>

<pre><code>else if (to == "Support" &amp;&amp; from == "Agent")
                {
                    response.Say("Connecting you to the first caller");
                    var queueDial = new Dial().Queue("Support");
                    response.Append(queueDial);
                }
</code></pre>

<p>That immediately connected me to my personal cell phone that was in the support queue.</p>

<p>By the way, in order to have the button call the <strong>client</strong> Support, I had to save my "Twiml App" name to Support.</p>

